# Getting Started

## Overview

You'll build a counter Agent and run it two ways: as plain data, then as a
live process. By the end, this works:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
signal = GettingStarted.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 4})
{:ok, committed, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, signal)
committed.state.count
# => 4
```

Along the way you'll use the four parts of Jido:

| Part | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| Agent definition | A module that declares a state schema and routes. |
| Agent instance | The definition plus an `id` and validated state. |
| Signal | A message. Its `type` selects one route, and the route runs one Action. |
| Agent Server | A process that owns one instance and commits each change. |

Run the cells from top to bottom. The whole notebook takes about two minutes.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

This also installs [jido_action](https://hexdocs.pm/jido_action/) and
[jido_signal](https://hexdocs.pm/jido_signal/), which provide Actions and
Signals.

## Define The Actions

An Agent does its work in Actions. Jido passes the current Agent state at
`context.agent_state`. Your Action returns the complete next state, not a
partial update:

```elixir
defmodule GettingStarted.Increment do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "increment",
    description: "Adds an amount to the counter",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{amount: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(%{amount: amount}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | count: state.count + amount}}
  end
end
```

An Action can also return a list of Directives. A Directive is work for the
runtime to do after it commits the state. `emit/2` asks for one outbound
Signal:

```elixir
defmodule GettingStarted.Reset do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "reset",
    description: "Sets the counter to zero and reports the previous value",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(_params, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | count: 0},
     [Jido.Agent.Directive.emit("counter.cleared", %{previous: state.count})]}
  end
end
```

`emit/2` returns data. It sends nothing.

## Declare The Agent

```elixir
defmodule GettingStarted.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "counter",
    description: "Counts increments"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/getting-started")

    route "counter.increment", GettingStarted.Increment,
      as: :increment,
      defaults: %{amount: 1} do
      state from: output()
    end

    route "counter.reset", GettingStarted.Reset, as: :reset do
      state from: output()
    end
  end
end
```

| Line | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `schema` | Validates the complete state. `Zoi.default(0)` sets the starting count. |
| `signal_source` | The `source` for Signals this Agent builds. |
| `route` | Maps one Signal type to one Action. The first matching route wins. |
| `defaults:` | Fills parameters that the Signal data leaves out. |
| `as: :increment` | Generates `increment_signal/2` and `increment_signal!/2`. |

Jido validates the declaration at compile time. A route to a module that
isn't an Action fails the build, not the first request.

## Create An Instance

The module gives you a definition, with no `id` and no `state`:

```elixir
definition = GettingStarted.Counter.definition()

{definition.name, definition.id, definition.state}
```

The result is `{"counter", nil, nil}`.

`instantiate!/1` adds an identity and validated state:

```elixir
agent = GettingStarted.Counter.instantiate!(id: "counter-1")

{agent.id, agent.state}
```

The result is `{"counter-1", %{count: 0}}`. Both values are `%Jido.Agent{}`
structs. A struct with an `id` but no `state`, or the reverse, is invalid.

## Run A Signal As Data

The generated constructor builds a Signal with the route's type and the
Agent's source:

```elixir
signal = GettingStarted.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 2})

{signal.type, signal.source, signal.data}
```

The result is `{"counter.increment", "/guides/getting-started", %{amount: 2}}`.

`cmd/2` routes the Signal, runs the Action, and validates the result:

```elixir
{:ok, candidate, directives} = GettingStarted.Counter.cmd(agent, signal)

{agent.state.count, candidate.state.count, directives}
```

The result is `{0, 2, []}`. `agent` is unchanged. `cmd/2` starts no process,
saves nothing, and runs no Directives.

With no data, the route default supplies `amount: 1`:

```elixir
signal = GettingStarted.Counter.increment_signal!()
{:ok, candidate, []} = GettingStarted.Counter.cmd(candidate, signal)

candidate.state.count
```

The result is `3`.

## Read A Directive

```elixir
reset = GettingStarted.Counter.reset_signal!()
{:ok, cleared, [emit]} = GettingStarted.Counter.cmd(candidate, reset)

{cleared.state.count, emit.signal.type, emit.signal.data}
```

The result is `{0, "counter.cleared", %{previous: 3}}`. Jido built a complete
Signal from the Agent's `signal_source`. It hasn't been sent.

## Handle Errors

Invalid data returns an exception struct and leaves the Agent unchanged:

```elixir
bad = GettingStarted.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: "two"})
{:error, error} = GettingStarted.Counter.cmd(agent, bad)

{error.__struct__, Exception.message(error)}
```

The result is
`{Jido.Action.Error.InvalidInputError, "invalid type: expected integer, at amount"}`.
Jido also logs a warning for the failed Action.

A Signal type with no route fails before any Action runs:

```elixir
unknown = Jido.Signal.new!("counter.unknown", %{}, source: "/guides/getting-started")
{:error, error} = GettingStarted.Counter.cmd(agent, unknown)

Exception.message(error)
```

The result is `"No route accepts this Signal type"`.

## Start A Live Agent

A live Agent runs under a Jido instance. `Jido.start/0` starts the default
instance for scripts and Livebook. In an application, you add your own Jido
module to your supervision tree.

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
```

```elixir
{:ok, counter} =
  Jido.start_agent(GettingStarted.Counter,
    id: "live-counter",
    default_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}
  )
```

`default_dispatch:` sends emitted Signals to this notebook's process. Without
it, the Server sends emitted Signals back to itself, which starts another Turn.
[Life of a Signal](signal-to-effects.md) covers the delivery rules.

## Send Signals

`Jido.AgentServer.call/3` runs one Turn and returns after the commit:

```elixir
signal = GettingStarted.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 4})
{:ok, committed, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, signal)

committed.state.count
```

The result is `4`. Read the committed state at any time:

```elixir
Jido.AgentServer.agent(counter).state.count
```

The result is `4`. `status/1` reports the revision and the last Turn:

```elixir
status = Jido.AgentServer.status(counter)

{status.state_version, status.last_outcome.status}
```

The result is `{1, :succeeded}`. Each committed Turn adds one to
`state_version`.

## Receive An Emit

The Server commits the reset, replies to `call/3`, then dispatches
`counter.cleared`:

```elixir
reset = GettingStarted.Counter.reset_signal!()
{:ok, committed, [_emit]} = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, reset)

receive do
  {:signal, %Jido.Signal{type: "counter.cleared"} = cleared} -> cleared.data
after
  1_000 -> :no_signal
end
```

The result is `%{previous: 4}`.

## Failed Turns

A failed Turn returns an error and keeps the last committed state:

```elixir
signal = GettingStarted.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 10})
{:ok, _committed, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, signal)

bad = GettingStarted.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: "ten"})
{:error, _error} = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, bad)

Jido.AgentServer.agent(counter).state.count
```

The result is `10`.

## Clean Up

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("live-counter")
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- An Agent module declares a schema and routes. It doesn't start a process.
- `instantiate!/1` turns a definition into an instance with an `id` and state.
- The first route matching the Signal type runs one Action.
- An Action returns the complete next state and, optionally, Directives.
- `cmd/2` returns a candidate and Directives without committing or sending.
- An Agent Server runs one Turn at a time. It commits, replies, then runs
  Directives.
- A failed Turn leaves the committed state unchanged.

